seg-473ed6ca71e5 = <c1/> fixed the population, the definitions and the bars before the first scored run; <c2/> records that run. Result: <b>the registered minimum-data rule failed</b> ({ "4" } scorable jamming windows from { "4" } of the { "12" } <c3/> folders: { "5" } folders were dropped by the baseline rule and { "3" } UBX logs state their start only as GPS time of week), so <b>no bar is assessable</b>. Nothing was loosened or re-run. <b>v2</b> (<c4/>, committed alone before its run; data handling only, same bars and minimum-data rule) placed the GPS-time-only logs from the folder date, scored the { "12" } CSV-only folders and used a moving-platform session for the moving rovers. Result (<c5/>): { "14" } of { "24" } folders kept but only <c6/> scorable jamming windows, so the minimum-data rule failed again and <b>no bar is assessable</b>. v1's result is unchanged. Descriptive only: { "3" } of { "7" } jamming windows alarmed within { "10" } s, { "4" } later, none missed; median latency { "18" } s; clean alarm rate { "0.089" }. The binding limit is now the baseline rule, which removes { "10" } folders; a further registration would need a different rule for early windows. The dataset (JammerTest { "2024" }, Zenodo { "15911589" }, GPL-3.0-or-later) is fetched by <c7/> and never vendored; the test <c8/> pins the recorded run when <c9/> points at the extracted dataset.

